ere they can actually  go and do it often the academic parts  not so challenging it's the social and  the unstructured part that is there are  problems that do remain it's difficult  in terms of understanding the cure  you'll find a lot of people who claim oh  my child was cured of autism over the  years seeing thousands upon thousands of  children probably I've seen five that if  I met the child I wouldn't necessarily  know that they had autism but that's  five out of so probably somewhere over  seven and 10,000 so I remember those now  it's not to say people don't do a lot  better but often there's some residual  problems which is not surprising this is  a very serious problem again and we'll  talk about what normal means and what  cure is treatment there's a very good  report we're going to talk about in two  weeks two weeks on treating children  with autism for the National Research  Council  it looks at all the programs around the  country that have empirical data to show  they work it's about ten of these  programs they're wonderful programs they  have some differences but many things in  common they all focus on structure  intensive intervention they have some  things going for them in terms of  working on the kinds of things  early intervention language  communication joint attention we'll have  a chance to talk about that they do make  the point in this report that not every  child gets better some kids even despite  good programs don't do so well and we  need to understand more about that as a  group children are doing better and  better and better well also have a  chance later on we'll talk about drug  treatments drug treatments don't cure  autism but they can help with some of  the symptoms and sometimes that actually  helps the child participate in program
 

